ZERO BOOTABLE DISK AND MENU VERIFICATION
2026-11-20

Final payload:18874368 bytes.
SHA256:006703b31f8d9a3a3302066da43fce2c07551eba4f87f4bb935165453d75eec6
Reference artifacts:/tmp/zero-support/validation-0.14/build-release/

The source-authored Zero native builder produced a new MBR/ESP disk payload
directly from original project artifacts. No ISO was read, renamed or attached
during these boot tests. Linux and Windows native builds use media/disk.zero.
Windows was executed under Wine; its full final output matches Linux byte
for byte, including the menus, firmware writers and current compiler bundle.

FINAL EXACT-PAYLOAD CHECKS:97 PASS

ACTUAL USB BOOT CHECKS:58
BIOS x64:15 checks. BIOS IA32:15 checks.
UEFI x64:14 checks. UEFI IA32:14 checks.
Each VM used QEMU xHCI plus USB mass storage, no CD or ISO drive. BIOS used
SeaBIOS; EFI used architecture-matching OVMF. Tests reached an interactive
terminal, ran info/help/write, formatted only a new private ATA data fixture,
saved/read a file, ran both bundled Zero and Ezero compilers and outputs,
then stopped and started a fresh VM and verified persistent fixture content.
BIOS tests additionally checked the CPU-selected kernel architecture.
The USB boot medium remained unchanged. All test VMs were stopped afterward.

INTEGRATED UEFI MENU CHECKS:6
Three named checks for x64 and three for IA32:
1 The new menu appears from the USB removable-media startup path.
2 Selecting2 starts the actual bundled Zero Flash firmware application;
  absent source media produces its controlled missing-source result.
3 Enter returns to our menu, then1 starts Zero OS and the terminal works.
The firmware shell is not required. The writer's actual raw-disk write and
source-selection checks are reported in its separate verification document.

INDEPENDENT STRUCTURAL AND FAILURE CHECKS:33
Twenty-nine checks validate the MBR, ESP, FAT16 chains, original kernel/tool
slots, menu/writer aliases and their byte-for-byte source artifacts, and
reject malformed, truncated, wrong-architecture or oversized inputs.
Header-only truncation is tested for all six EFI artifacts. Existing output
files are preserved. Four additional regressions verify the linked-bootstrap
bound:946-byte input ends its jump at439 and is accepted;947 and952 bytes are
rejected. A concurrent append after readback begins is detected with exit74.
The failed new output is retained with an inspection diagnostic; no pathname
unlink is attempted after closing a handle, avoiding replacement-file deletion.

REVIEW FIXES INCLUDED IN THE FINAL CHECKED SOURCES
The bootstrap size bound protects the MBR disk signature at440. Readback
checks EOF and final size after comparing all expected bytes. Each EFI raw
section must fit its actual input file before slot padding. Failed outputs
are retained rather than unlinked by a potentially replaced pathname.
The menu invokes StartImage on a separately allocated1MiB continuation stack.
This fixed IA32 firmware corruption caused by nesting two64KiB Zero arenas
on the small firmware-provided stack. Both application return and OS startup
were rechecked after that fix on both UEFI architectures.

